```
@id("close-workorder-requires-recent-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: Drupe::get_approval_record_Output_decision::"SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_kind: Drupe::get_approval_record_Output_approver_kind::"OPERATOR"
  }
};

// Read-only lookups. get_approval_record must be permitted so that its
// response is recorded and can serve as the confirmation of an approval.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_approval_record",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);
```

These policies rest on one assumption: that `get_approval_record` is the lookup that confirms an approval. Its output carries enumerated `decision` and `approver_kind` values (`SIGNED_OFF`, `OPERATOR`), so the policy can match them exactly. The `authority_get_approval` tool returns free-form strings with no documented values, so I allow it only as a read-only lookup and do not use it as the proof.

- **Closing:** `close_workorder` is allowed only when `site_id` is `"MAIN"`. It also needs a `get_approval_record` response from this session within the last hour, for the same `wonum` and `site_id`. That response must show `decision` as `SIGNED_OFF` and `approver_kind` as `OPERATOR`.
- **Failed lookups:** only `::response` events count. A lookup that failed or was denied is recorded as an error and confirms nothing, and a declined, queued or bot/scheduler approval does not match.
- **Approving:** `approve_workorder` has no permit, so it is denied by default. The same applies to every other write tool (`update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`, `generate_work_order`). The agent therefore cannot approve its own work, directly or indirectly.

I wrote the enum literals as entity references, e.g. `Drupe::get_approval_record_Output_decision::"SIGNED_OFF"`, because the schema declares those fields as enum types rather than strings. If your validator expects plain string literals there, swap them in.
